WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.640
Today we are going to talk&nbsp;
about video stabilization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.160
A typical application of this is when we&nbsp;
record family memories, and other cool events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.360
and sometimes, the footage gets so shaky&nbsp;
that we barely know what is going on.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.560
In these cases, video stabilization&nbsp;
techniques can come to the rescue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:30.800
which means that in goes a shaky video,&nbsp;
and out comes a smooth video. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.840
that is easier said than done. Despite&nbsp;
many years of progress, there is a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:42.320
selection of previous methods that can do that,&nbsp;
however, they suffer from one of two issues.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.600
Issue number one is cropping. This&nbsp;
means that we get usable results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.160
but we have to pay a great price for it, which is,&nbsp;
cropping away a great deal of the video content.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.680
Issue number two is when we get the&nbsp;
entirety of the video, no cropping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:08.480
however, the price to be paid for this is that we&nbsp;
get lots of issues that we call visual artifacts.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.720
Unfortunately, today, when we&nbsp;
stabilize, we have to choose our poison.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:21.440
It’s either cropping, or artifacts. Which one&nbsp;
would you choose? That is difficult to decide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:28.000
of course, because none of these two tradeoffs are&nbsp;
great. So our question today is, can we do better?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.440
Well, the Law of Papers says that of course, just&nbsp;
one or two more papers down the line, this will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.280
way better. So, let’s see…this is&nbsp;
what this new method is capable of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.520
Hold on to your papers, and notice that this will&nbsp;
indeed be a full-size video, so we already know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:57.440
that probably there will be artifacts. But…wait&nbsp;
a second! No artifacts. Whoa. How can that be?

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:03.120
What does this new method do that previous&nbsp;
techniques didn’t? These magical results are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.760
combination of several things: one, the new method&nbsp;
can estimate the motion of these objects better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:17.120
two, it removes blurred images from the videos&nbsp;
and three, collects data from neighboring video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.400
frames more effectively. This leads to a greater&nbsp;
understanding of the video it is looking at.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.520
Now, of course, not even this technique is&nbsp;
perfect - rapid camera motion may lead to warping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.320
and if you look carefully, you may find some&nbsp;
artifacts, usually around the sides of the screen.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:43.920
So far, we looked at previous methods, and the&nbsp;
new method. It seems better. That’s great. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:50.240
how do we measure which one is better? Do we&nbsp;
just look? An even harder question would be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.840
if the new method is indeed better,&nbsp;
okay, but by how much better is it?

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.360
Let’s try to answer all of these questions. We&nbsp;
can evaluate these techniques against each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:08.000
in three different ways. One, we can look at the&nbsp;
footage ourselves. We have already done that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.880
and we had to tightly hold on to our&nbsp;
papers, it has done quite well in this test.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.920
Test number two is a quantitative test. In other&nbsp;
words, we can mathematically define how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:26.960
distortion there is in an output video, how smooth&nbsp;
it is, and more, and compare the output videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.880
based on these metrics. In many cases, these&nbsp;
previous techniques are quite close to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:41.440
and now, let’s unveil the new method.&nbsp;
Whoa. It scored best or second best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:48.240
on 6 out of 8 tests. This is truly remarkable,&nbsp;
especially given that some of these competitors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:55.440
are from less than a year ago. That is nimble&nbsp;
progress in machine learning research. Loving it.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.760
And the third way to test which technique is&nbsp;
better, and by how much is by conducting a user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:08.480
study. The authors have done that too! In this,&nbsp;
46 humans were called in, were shown the shaky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:15.040
input video, the result of a previous method, and&nbsp;
the new method, and were asked three questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:22.400
Which video preserves the most content, which has&nbsp;
fewer imperfections, and which is more stable.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.480
And the results were stunning - despite&nbsp;
looking at many different competing techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.400
the participants found the new method to be&nbsp;
better at the very least 60% of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:42.640
on all three questions. In some cases, even&nbsp;
90% of the time or higher. Praise the papers!

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:49.200
Now, there is only one question left. If it&nbsp;
is so much better than previous techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:54.800
how much longer does it take to run?
With one exception, these previous methods take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:02.560
from half a second to about 7.5 seconds per frame,&nbsp;
and this new one asks for 9.5 seconds per frame.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:11.040
And in return, it creates these absolutely amazing&nbsp;
results. So, from this glorious day on, fewer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:17.360
or maybe no important memories will be lost&nbsp;
due to camera shaking. What a time to be alive!

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.760
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

